":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\"blind_id\":\"C008\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"Never says Rule 6c-11 permits the AP agreement to be with the ETF or a service provider. It says only that the rule 'doesn't prescribe AP signatories; fund contracts do.'\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"Does not present the AP and distributor as main counterparties, transfer-agent acceptance/countersignature, or the trust as third-party beneficiary signing specified provisions. It instead names the fund/trust and AP as the parties, with the distributor only 'often' added.\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"States that the ICAV, as a corporate body, signs AP agreements, which partly covers direct ICAV signature. It omits signing with each AP on behalf of sub-funds, and its contrast with the US rests on a wrong US pattern (trust signing via trustees).\"}],\"central_error\":\"'Typically the fund, acting through its trust, and the Authorized Participant are the parties, often with the distributor' and 'US ETFs sign via a trust's trustees' match the central error 'Reviewed US trust normally principal AP counterparty instead of distributor/beneficiary distinction'.\",\"local_error\":\"'It doesn't prescribe AP signatories; fund contracts do.' This misdescribes Rule 6c-11, which addresses an agreement with the ETF or its service provider.\",\"grade\":\"wrong\",\"rationale\":\"The answer explicitly makes the US trust the principal AP counterparty, which is a listed central error and forces 'wrong'. Coverage is also below half (0.5 of 3): point 1 is missed, point 2 is missed or contradicted, and point 3 is only partly covered.\"}\n```","ttft_ms":4028,"type":"result","duration_ms":8517,"uuid":"15ea2f57-52f8-4b12-9940-9b65ff511aba","ttft_stream_ms":1453,"time_to_request_ms":93,"first_content_frame_ms":1453,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
